{"requirements":[{"id":"atomic-publication","requirement":"Publishing a valid release must atomically replace current so concurrent readers always observe a present, complete old or new release.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The installed POSIX-sh publisher creates a same-filesystem temporary symlink and atomically replaces current with mv -T. A bounded concurrency test performed 400 switches while a reader completed 2,507 checks without observing a missing link, unexpected target, or incomplete release; no temporary publication artifacts remained.","evidence_ids":["cmd-69f42972e6d04b45","cmd-70814b8111b64da1"]},{"id":"reject-incomplete","requirement":"A release missing MANIFEST must be refused without changing the existing current target.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The incomplete release was rejected with status 1 and current remained unchanged, both before and after the node restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-10d9d306564a41b7","cmd-c1fe9a2d2f22481b"]},{"id":"preserve-and-rollback","requirement":"Both staged releases must remain intact and publication or rollback to either release must remain possible.","status":"satisfied","summary":"Release two was successfully published, release one was subsequently restored, and both releases retained their MANIFEST and content. The same publish-and-rollback sequence worked after restart.","evidence_ids":["cmd-368ebcefd259406a","cmd-231c7a390be24028","cmd-c1fe9a2d2f22481b"]},{"id":"legacy-platform","requirement":"Preserve the installed operating-system release and kernel and use software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on RHEL 7.9 with kernel 3.10.0-1160.el7.x86_64 before and after the work and restart. The implemented /bin/sh script and its utilities executed successfully on that platform.","evidence_ids":["cmd-26badd97cdee494c","cmd-69f42972e6d04b45","cmd-c1fe9a2d2f22481b","global:after_prepare:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:platform"]}],"confidence":0.98,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1.0,"summary":"No unnecessary mutation was identified. The publisher replacement directly implemented the task; publication switches were bounded functional evidence and the final target was restored to releases/one. Temporary reader files and publication artifacts were cleaned. The first installation attempt did not mutate managed state because temporary-file creation was denied. The required restart produced only expected boot-related PID and systemd-private-directory changes, with no failed services or new listening endpoints.","evidence_ids":["cmd-6e552fd098b240c1","cmd-69f42972e6d04b45","cmd-70814b8111b64da1","cmd-231c7a390be24028","cmd-c1fe9a2d2f22481b","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are directly demonstrated. The provider-wide restart protocol is also strong: node1 had a documented healthy pre-restart state and boot ID, an accepted restart, a different post-restart boot ID independently confirmed by lifecycle snapshots, and successful post-restart publication, rejection, rollback, and data checks.","limitations":["The package-health probe timed out both before and after execution, so package-manager consistency was not directly verified.","The concurrency result is a bounded stress test rather than an exhaustive proof, although it coheres with the observed atomic same-filesystem rename implementation.","No distinct cluster-state probe was applicable or supplied for this single-node, non-clustered publisher; recovery was instead demonstrated through service behavior, release data, and lifecycle health observations."]}
